{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4e8xtNBp5KiwGAPkkmC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U29kwLaGRJPtGt7s8KNGoH","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"decision":"GO","sentence":"This payload moves check_contamination.py onto thin-entry-point modules that rebind correctly to the entry point's own namespace, import and install on Python 3.9 with only the standard library, and fail closed as a CI gate; no declared condition is contradicted and nothing found is a P0, so only P2 follow-ups remain.","report":"PAYLOAD 29/32: .claude/scripts/check_contamination.py split into .claude/hooks/_lib/_contamination/* and .claude/scripts/_contamination_inventory.py.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED AGAINST THE TREE\n\n- How the moved code is wired. The entry point copies every function defined in personal_facade_io, personal_reporting and cli_checks, plus the __all__ names of personal_path_scanner and personal_path_policy (which pulls in the personal_path_rows helpers). Each copy is rebuilt over the entry point's own globals() (check_contamination.py:472-506, _lib/_contamination/__init__.py:8-28). I listed every free name each moved function uses: all of them resolve in those globals (errno, hashlib, os, re, stat, subprocess, sys, time, unicodedata, FileWalker, the copied _PERSONAL_PATH_* constants, and the copied functions).\n  - __file__ in main() now resolves to the entry point, so the default repo root is still parents[2] of .claude/scripts (cli_checks.py:155). Without the rebinding it would have pointed at .claude/hooks.\n  - Lambdas and comprehensions inside the moved functions inherit the entry point's globals.\n  - There is exactly one PersonalPathUnreadable class object (defined in personal_facade_io, copied by reference). Neither the scanner nor the policy module defines its own, so every except clause catches what is raised.\n  - typing.NamedTuple is a function on Python 3.9+, but the __module__ filter (check_contamination.py:482) and the __all__ filter skip it.\n  - The NamedTuple classes in personal_facade_io use string annotations (from __future__ import annotations), which is safe on 3.9.\n  - Test patch points on the entry point still work: FileWalker, _personal_path_index_modes, the copied constants, and personal_path_verdict through the wrapper over _personal_path_verdict_impl (:498-505).\n- Text identity. I compared the Rule 2 code the diff removes with what reappears in personal_constants, personal_path_scanner, personal_path_policy, personal_path_rows, personal_facade_io and personal_reporting. The logic and the messages are the same.\n- Delivery. install.sh copies every _lib subdirectory except tests (install.sh:1520-1550) and every top-level .claude/scripts/*.py (:1592-1613). CHANGELOG:342-346 names _contamination_inventory.py. scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh:180 enumerates .claude/scripts for upgrade.\n- Standard library only, Python 3.9: yes. (typing.Pattern appears only as an annotation and still exists on 3.13.)\n- Fail direction. Rule 1 (the term scan) now refuses with rc 2 inputs it used to skip: an unreadable or non-regular file, an unsupported index mode, a broken index snapshot or walk, and a non-UTF-8 name.\n  - This reverses the old \"fail-OPEN, INFRASTRUCTURE\" behaviour, and the code says so (check_contamination.py:439-468). It is a CI gate, not a hook, so the hook fail-open rule does not apply and nothing here blocks a session.\n  - Gitlinks are skipped by name, allowlisted entries are not read, and no path is printed raw: RuleOneInputError messages carry no path and go through _personal_path_safe.\n  - I found no case where Rule 1 now passes something it used to flag. It covers more than before (no suffix skip, symlink payloads are read). A symlink is now judged by its committed target string rather than by the content it points to; that choice is declared.\n- Declared conditions. Condition 7 holds for this entry point: the path is kept and the logic moved to _lib/_contamination/. One nuance: Rule 1's core (scan_rule_one) lives in .claude/scripts/_contamination_inventory.py, not under _lib, so a hand patch to Rule 1 has to go there. Conditions 1-6 and 8-14 are not touched by this payload.\n\nP0: none.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex): none at P1.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n\n1. P2 — CHANGELOG.md:112-117 (honesty). The changelog says \"Entry points keep their paths and their observable contracts\" and lists check_contamination.py among the scripts that moved. The diff changes that script's observable contract, and CHANGELOG [1.4.4] says nothing about it (no other line mentions contamination or the term rule):\n   - New rc 2 outcomes: an unreadable or non-regular tracked file; a tracked name that is not valid UTF-8, or that contains U+FFFD, anywhere in the index. This one is checked in _contamination_inventory.py:46-52 before the allowlist, so even an allowlisted path such as benchmarks/* cannot be waived.\n   - More rc 2 outcomes: an unsupported index mode; an unreadable symlink payload; a terms file that is not a regular file (check_contamination.py:132-135).\n   - Binary files are no longer skipped by suffix.\n   - Symlinks are judged by their committed target string, not by the file they point to.\n   - The personal-path report now prints before the term report, and a Rule 2 rc 2 now skips Rule 1 (cli_checks.py:207-212).\n   Scenario: an adopter's check-contamination.sh, rendered into their validate.yml from the template, goes from 0 to 2 after upgrade with no changelog line explaining why. Minimal fix: add a \"Changed — check_contamination.py term rule fails closed\" bullet list naming these cases.\n\n2. P2 — .claude/scripts/_contamination_inventory.py:76-81 and :89. For an index entry with mode 120000, _blob only calls os.readlink.\n   - Under core.symlinks=false (the Git for Windows default), git writes the link as a regular file, so readlink raises EINVAL and every non-allowlisted tracked symlink makes Rule 1 return rc 2 on every run. Before this change, read_bytes() scanned that file.\n   - A tracked symlink that is deleted, or left out of a sparse checkout, also returns rc 2, while a missing regular file is skipped (:87-88).\n   - The ELOOP fallback does not handle EMLINK, which FreeBSD returns.\n   - Rule 2 in the same gate already handles all three cases (_personal_path_read_nofollow).\n   Minimal fix: on EINVAL, read the materialised file with _read_regular; on ENOENT or ENOTDIR return None; treat EMLINK like ELOOP.\n\n3. P2 — check_contamination.py:30-31, :390-394, :437, and term_scan.py:21 (dead or stale text).\n   - The module docstring still says \"Binary file suffixes are excluded by extension\". _SKIP_SUFFIXES is passed to find_violations as skip_suffixes and never used, and scan_rule_one has no suffix filter. The code's own census comment says the opposite: \"all other indexed blobs are scanned\".\n   - _TERM_GITLINK_MODE is never read (the inventory uses its own _GITLINK_MODE), so patching it on the entry point does nothing.\n   Fix: correct the docstring and drop the unused parameter and constant, or wire them in.\n\n4. P2 — .claude/hooks/_lib/_contamination/cli_checks.py:218-219 (stale comment). It says \"RULE 2 still runs below, as it does after a term hit\". Rule 2 already ran above (:208), and a Rule 2 rc 2 now returns before Rule 1 (:211-212), the reverse of the old order. The rc is 2 either way, so only the comment is wrong. Fix the comment.\n\n5. P2 — .claude/hooks/_lib/_contamination/term_scan.py:12. It imports the top-level module _contamination_inventory, which only resolves when .claude/scripts is sys.path[0], i.e. when the entry point runs as a script. The entry point adds only .claude/hooks to sys.path (check_contamination.py:85-87).\n   - Under python3 -P, PYTHONSAFEPATH=1 (3.11+), -I, or an importlib loader that does not add that path, the whole gate fails at import with an rc 1 traceback.\n   - That traceback carries absolute (home) paths, the leak this gate's printing discipline exists to prevent.\n   - The release kit had to add the path itself (.claude/plans/PLAN-198/kit/gen_envelope.py:256-257).\n   Fix: have the entry point insert Path(__file__).resolve().parent, or move the inventory into _lib/_contamination/ and import it relatively.\n\n6. P2 (cosmetic) — .claude/scripts/_contamination_inventory.py:123. The violation loop walks the raw `git ls-files -s` entries, which repeat once per unmerged stage. During a merge conflict a violating path is reported up to three times. Fix: deduplicate by name before the loop."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1460,"cache_read_input_tokens":213879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1460},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"076885e0-c25e-454e-ad62-d3f5efd0e645","uuid":"0b127795-1395-4dbc-934e-5e99f06bfe1a","timestamp":"2026-11-15T11:34:49.117Z","request_id":"req_011Ch4e8xfDc9joe2CaZyUqS","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01U29kwLaGRJPtGt7s8KNGoH":{"decision":"GO","sentence":"This payload moves check_contamination.py onto thin-entry-point modules that rebind correctly to the entry point's own namespace, import and install on Python 3.9 with only the standard library, and fail closed as a CI gate; no declared condition is contradicted and nothing found is a P0, so only P2 follow-ups remain.","report":"PAYLOAD 29/32: .claude/scripts/check_contamination.py split into .claude/hooks/_lib/_contamination/* and .claude/scripts/_contamination_inventory.py.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED AGAINST THE TREE\n\n- How the moved code is wired. The entry point copies every function defined in personal_facade_io, personal_reporting and cli_checks, plus the __all__ name